Didn't have much content, it was only on
Windows PCs, it was very small scale.

00:15:23.154 --> 00:15:25.153
And then we just kept working at it.

00:15:25.153 --> 00:15:29.094
And we finally got the Starz deal, which
was a epic deal to be able to carry.

00:15:29.094 --> 00:15:30.887
All the, the Starz movies.

00:15:30.887 --> 00:15:33.374
&gt;&gt; Hm-mm.
&gt;&gt; And got the Xbox deal to get on the TV,

00:15:33.374 --> 00:15:37.075
that was in 2008 and then the thing really
started taking off.

00:15:37.075 --> 00:15:40.211
And then we finished, finished, but we got
ahead in streaming and

00:15:40.211 --> 00:15:41.893
we were still a domestic business.

00:15:41.893 --> 00:15:47.570
Now this was 2010, so we've been 13 years
just in the US to get to streaming.

00:15:47.570 --> 00:15:51.961
But now with streaming, you know, we could
expand internationally without having to

00:15:51.961 --> 00:15:56.371
deal with, you know, British post and
Canada post and, you know, French post.

00:15:56.371 --> 00:16:00.354
So we started that expansion very actively
in the last four years which is

00:16:00.354 --> 00:16:03.254
going from a domestic company to global
and it's hard.

00:16:03.254 --> 00:16:04.545
Right.
You've got a lot of

00:16:04.545 --> 00:16:09.045
things that you didn't realize that are
very locked into domestic after 13 years.

00:16:09.045 --> 00:16:10.475
And then the expansion into new and

00:16:10.475 --> 00:16:13.192
original content, which is like other,
other cable networks.

00:16:13.192 --> 00:16:16.372
So if you look at FX, if you look at HBO,
AMC.

00:16:16.372 --> 00:16:19.558
They all start on other people's license
content and

00:16:19.558 --> 00:16:23.187
then try their hand at producing original
content.

00:16:23.187 --> 00:16:25.613
And so that's been obviously a huge
success for us.

00:16:25.613 --> 00:16:28.679
So the, the big hard challenges over the
next five and

00:16:28.679 --> 00:16:32.986
ten years are getting more global and
getting more into original content.

00:16:32.986 --> 00:16:35.134
So we're working on both of those.

00:16:35.134 --> 00:16:40.892
&gt;&gt; Is it hard to convince the original on
your first studio deals?

00:16:40.892 --> 00:16:45.009
How hard was it to convince them that, you
know, streaming was going to be okay and

00:16:45.009 --> 00:16:46.988
that their rights would be protected?

00:16:46.988 --> 00:16:49.075
And that there was an economic model for

00:16:49.075 --> 00:16:53.325
them, cuz you really changed the way they
think about their business, right?

00:16:53.325 --> 00:16:55.359
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
The very early deals were with

00:16:55.359 --> 00:17:00.519
minimum guarantees to them that killed us,
but, you know, we just had to pay it.

00:17:00.519 --> 00:17:03.388
But then we were able to grow out of that.

00:17:03.388 --> 00:17:05.488
And of course, they had seen the movie and

00:17:05.488 --> 00:17:08.069
television business had seen the music
business.

00:17:08.069 --> 00:17:10.810
So they knew keeping the head in the sand
was not the right approach.

00:17:10.810 --> 00:17:15.854
They, they built their own pay-per-view
service, an iTunes competitor called

00:17:15.854 --> 00:17:20.550
Movie Link and they started that in 2001,
way before there was a market.

00:17:20.550 --> 00:17:23.630
And so, you know, that media group, movies
and

00:17:23.630 --> 00:17:27.829
TVs did not want to be like the brothers
in the music business.

00:17:27.829 --> 00:17:31.054
So there was a lot of motivation to
experiment and, and

00:17:31.054 --> 00:17:34.504
then we became another bidder, where we
bid against HBO.

00:17:34.504 --> 00:17:37.267
And so the artists do better, because it's
raising the price.

00:17:37.267 --> 00:17:41.569
The con, content owners do better because
we're another bidder.

00:17:41.569 --> 00:17:44.975
&gt;&gt; They probably at, at first thought that
you were the the devil and

00:17:44.975 --> 00:17:48.640
now you're, you're the knight in shining
armor with the big checkbook.

00:17:48.640 --> 00:17:50.313
&gt;&gt; And they still think we're the,

00:17:50.313 --> 00:17:54.242
they always want more, you know, it's a
normal supplier relationship.

00:17:54.242 --> 00:17:55.487
And now, you know,

00:17:55.487 --> 00:18:00.341
with originals were sort of going around
them to developing brands on our own.

00:18:00.341 --> 00:18:04.265
And the big difference with original
content it's still a third

00:18:04.265 --> 00:18:06.981
party that's actually on set and on
location.

00:18:06.981 --> 00:18:12.323
And we're not on set that much but we do
the brand creation when it launches.

00:18:12.323 --> 00:18:16.201
So you have to take something like Orange
Is the New Black, which is unknown or

00:18:16.201 --> 00:18:20.020
Marco Polo that we have coming out in
December and it's an unknown series and

00:18:20.020 --> 00:18:21.829
then make it really big and give it.

00:18:21.829 --> 00:18:26.647
So the big change is us launching,
creating global brands.

00:18:26.647 --> 00:18:27.157
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:18:27.157 --> 00:18:29.143
&gt;&gt; And then it's a mix of the talent, you
know,

00:18:29.143 --> 00:18:30.989
the history, the storyline, all that.

00:18:30.989 --> 00:18:33.875
&gt;&gt; So how did you decide to, to get into
content?

00:18:33.875 --> 00:18:37.421
You know, up to this point you're really
primarily a distribution company.

00:18:37.421 --> 00:18:39.976
Is it a hard leap to go on a content and,
and

00:18:39.976 --> 00:18:43.563
how did you convince your board that it
was the right idea?

00:18:43.563 --> 00:18:47.134
&gt;&gt; Yeah one of our long term executives
been with us since 2000,

00:18:47.134 --> 00:18:49.565
Ted Sarandos really gets all the credit
for it.

00:18:49.565 --> 00:18:53.889
He's the one who talked the rest of us
that here's what cable networks do and

00:18:53.889 --> 00:18:56.792
look at this, and how they grow into
originals.

00:18:56.792 --> 00:19:01.407
And back in 2010 is when we launched our
commission, the first one,

00:19:01.407 --> 00:19:05.597
House of Cards, which is even before the
whole quick start incident.

00:19:05.597 --> 00:19:07.420
And so, it definitely helped us.

00:19:07.420 --> 00:19:08.122
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:19:08.122 --> 00:19:10.269
&gt;&gt; but, they're, they're long legs so it
was,

00:19:10.269 --> 00:19:13.886
you know, four years ago when we had to
make the decision on, on House of Cards.

00:19:13.886 --> 00:19:14.884
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:19:14.884 --> 00:19:17.219
And your success rate's quite high.

00:19:17.219 --> 00:19:20.914
I mean, you've had 31 nominations 7
Emmy's.

00:19:20.914 --> 00:19:24.823
And last year alone, you had 14
nominations and 3 Emmy's.

00:19:24.823 --> 00:19:28.201
I mean, by any metric a very high hit
rate, you know?

00:19:28.201 --> 00:19:32.844
What's your secret and, and what are you
gonna do when you've gotta flop?

00:19:32.844 --> 00:19:36.063
&gt;&gt; I am, you know, look, if we don't push
hard enough, you know?

00:19:36.063 --> 00:19:39.071
We should, we need to have a flop every
now and then.

00:19:39.071 --> 00:19:44.606
You know, it's somewhat the advantage of
being on a demand creation platform.

00:19:44.606 --> 00:19:48.991
And so Hemlock Row, we just commissioned
season three.

00:19:48.991 --> 00:19:50.456
Lilyhammer, we did season three.

00:19:50.456 --> 00:19:53.489
So we've never had a cancelled series in
our history.

00:19:53.489 --> 00:19:57.643
You know, we've ended some in season three
as opposed to carrying it for, for,

00:19:57.643 --> 00:19:59.304
you know, five or ten seasons.

00:19:59.304 --> 00:20:01.683
But it wraps up nicely in the storyline.

00:20:01.683 --> 00:20:05.880
The, you know, consumers are not left
hanging of you know, like Jericho or

00:20:05.880 --> 00:20:07.257
Firefly or these things.

00:20:07.257 --> 00:20:08.046
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:20:08.046 --> 00:20:10.983
&gt;&gt; So we're really excited by that track
record.

00:20:10.983 --> 00:20:14.120
But a lot of the advantage is because
we're online and,

00:20:14.120 --> 00:20:18.285
you know, its just much more flexible as
all of you know that are members.

00:20:18.285 --> 00:20:21.094
Cuz you can choose to watch when and how
you want.

00:20:21.094 --> 00:20:23.160
&gt;&gt; And how did you decide to go into
content?

00:20:23.160 --> 00:20:26.400
I mean, what, what, what was the, what
would you,

00:20:26.400 --> 00:20:29.401
what did you hope to achieve by going in?

00:20:29.401 --> 00:20:34.057
&gt;&gt; It's a moving of the brand when you're
not doing original content, you're,

00:20:34.057 --> 00:20:35.316
you're just iTunes.

00:20:35.316 --> 00:20:39.355
I mean, you carry other people's content
and you don't even have a full selection.

00:20:39.355 --> 00:20:42.363
And, you know, it's a commodity
relationship.

00:20:42.363 --> 00:20:46.044
You know, you subscribe to Netflix if it's
the lowest, cheapest price.

00:20:46.044 --> 00:20:49.908
And then when you develop a new brand like
Orange is the New Black,

00:20:49.908 --> 00:20:51.534
then people talk about it.

00:20:51.534 --> 00:20:52.974
The brand's closely associated.

00:20:52.974 --> 00:20:55.453
They join, because of the show.

00:20:55.453 --> 00:20:58.337
It's a much stronger and you've created
that brand.

00:20:58.337 --> 00:21:01.297
So you, you own the brand equity in that.

00:21:01.297 --> 00:21:05.616
So it's it's a much stronger relationship
with key content.

00:21:05.616 --> 00:21:10.083
And then, you know, next year, we have an
amazing new set of shows in addition to

00:21:10.083 --> 00:21:13.081
additional seasons that our current show
is coming out.

00:21:13.081 --> 00:21:14.800
So it just builds on itself.

00:21:14.800 --> 00:21:15.600
&gt;&gt; Hm.

00:21:15.600 --> 00:21:17.023
That's great.

00:21:17.023 --> 00:21:18.159
Did you, did you,

00:21:18.159 --> 00:21:23.078
were there any success models you were
looking at in terms of developing content?

00:21:23.078 --> 00:21:25.671
And, and how do you decide which,

00:21:25.671 --> 00:21:31.024
how many pieces of content to develop and
what is a good brand?

00:21:31.024 --> 00:21:34.023
&gt;&gt; [COUGH] Ted's team analyzes lots of
data and

00:21:34.023 --> 00:21:39.851
then at the day has to make a gut call
based upon the team that's together on it,

00:21:39.851 --> 00:21:44.218
how they think about it, does he have a
good feeling about it?

00:21:44.218 --> 00:21:46.237
But ultimately, it's a, it's a gut call.

00:21:46.237 --> 00:21:46.878
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; And

00:21:46.878 --> 00:21:51.085
then we're able to use differential
information to successfully promote that

00:21:51.085 --> 00:21:55.177
series to the right people, because almost
no series is good for everybody.

00:21:55.177 --> 00:22:00.146
So we've got tremendous leverage in
creating consumption and

00:22:00.146 --> 00:22:01.898
enjoyment of content.

00:22:01.898 --> 00:22:06.119
But in the upfront commissioning process
it's, you know,

00:22:06.119 --> 00:22:10.816
not hugely different from the kind of
people that do it at, at HBO and FX.

00:22:10.816 --> 00:22:11.652
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:22:11.652 --> 00:22:15.331
&gt;&gt; We tend, we tend to trust the artists
more.

00:22:15.331 --> 00:22:18.316
We tend to work by the freedom and
responsibility model more and

00:22:18.316 --> 00:22:19.427
they like that.

00:22:19.427 --> 00:22:24.212
But so for the artist point of view, we've
got a great reputation.

00:22:24.212 --> 00:22:25.037
&gt;&gt; Hm.

00:22:25.037 --> 00:22:29.269
So before we go back and, and, and talk a
little bit more about a little bit

00:22:29.269 --> 00:22:33.454
about management style, you know stay on
the content for a second.

00:22:33.454 --> 00:22:38.564
You, you kind of created this concept of
binge viewing that, that seemingly has,

00:22:38.564 --> 00:22:42.288
has altered people's the way people
consume content.

00:22:42.288 --> 00:22:43.223
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Can you talk a little bit

00:22:43.223 --> 00:22:43.728
more about that?

00:22:43.728 --> 00:22:46.630
What do you, how did you decide, did you
plan for that?

00:22:46.630 --> 00:22:50.368
And, and how do you exploit that and?

00:22:50.368 --> 00:22:53.436
&gt;&gt; No, really binge viewing, we
counter-planned on it,

00:22:53.436 --> 00:22:55.371
because we tried to kill the term.

00:22:55.371 --> 00:22:59.608
because, you know, binge is associated
with, you know, negative things really.

00:22:59.608 --> 00:23:01.455
And we thought this wasn't good.

00:23:01.455 --> 00:23:04.937
And what we didn't realize is that the
term would come to be

00:23:04.937 --> 00:23:09.630
gentler interpreted and the fact that it
was a little bit aggressive [UNKNOWN],

00:23:09.630 --> 00:23:11.068
you know, catch fire.

00:23:11.068 --> 00:23:13.227
But in the first real binge viewing was
DVD.

00:23:13.227 --> 00:23:16.194
TV sets on DVD and people would get
Sopranos and

00:23:16.194 --> 00:23:19.553
The Wire and Mad Men on DVD and watched
ten episodes.

00:23:19.553 --> 00:23:24.267
So that we felt the behavior just on DVD
shipments going to TV series.

00:23:24.267 --> 00:23:28.006
And then when you brought that online, it
really accelerated it.

00:23:28.006 --> 00:23:32.054
And, you know, when you think about it
when you buy a book, you know, ten or

00:23:32.054 --> 00:23:33.590
fifteen chapters in a book.

00:23:33.590 --> 00:23:37.298
Then it's not remarkable that you're
allowed to read three chapters one night,

00:23:37.298 --> 00:23:39.673
one the next night and five chapters the
next night.

00:23:39.673 --> 00:23:40.573
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; You know,

00:23:40.573 --> 00:23:43.603
that basic thing of human control of
watching on your schedule,

00:23:43.603 --> 00:23:45.524
instead of on the programmer schedule.

00:23:45.524 --> 00:23:47.475
In hind sight, it's pretty basic.

00:23:47.475 --> 00:23:51.431
So linear TV as we know it will be as much
as a relic in 20 years as

00:23:51.431 --> 00:23:56.752
the fixed line telephone, you know, like
some of your parents will still have one.

00:23:56.752 --> 00:24:01.290
But, you know, most people it, you know,
it's, it's pretty irrelevant for.

00:24:01.290 --> 00:24:02.254
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:24:02.254 --> 00:24:05.853
So, so let's, let's talk a little bit
about management style and philosophy.

00:24:05.853 --> 00:24:08.755
And, you know, yours is seemingly has
changed and

00:24:08.755 --> 00:24:10.788
evolved over the years, you know?

00:24:10.788 --> 00:24:12.331
If you don't mind, talk a little bit about
that.

00:24:12.331 --> 00:24:18.914
You know, you spec especially talk about
your concept of freedom of responsibility.

00:24:18.914 --> 00:24:23.569
&gt;&gt; Yeah, up until 2002, all we focused on
was survival.

00:24:23.569 --> 00:24:25.412
And then once we got public and, and

00:24:25.412 --> 00:24:29.106
we're profitable we started thinking, wow,
this thing might last.

00:24:29.106 --> 00:24:32.832
And the, the cruelest irony would be to
not want to work there.

00:24:32.832 --> 00:24:35.330
You know to let it become a place we
didn't want to be.

00:24:35.330 --> 00:24:37.592
And so we tried to think through what do
we really care about?

00:24:37.592 --> 00:24:40.660
We realized what we really care about is
working with incredible people on

00:24:40.660 --> 00:24:42.644
hard problems and that, that was the
essence.

00:24:42.644 --> 00:24:46.694
So we should organize everything around
supporting that of working with, you know,

00:24:46.694 --> 00:24:48.390
just incredibly talented people.

00:24:48.390 --> 00:24:49.878
And so that led us to freedom and

00:24:49.878 --> 00:24:53.671
responsibility, because incredible people
don't wanna be micro managed.

00:24:53.671 --> 00:24:58.410
And the idea is that manage through
setting context and letting people run.

00:24:58.410 --> 00:25:00.981
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; You've gotta have the right people,

00:25:00.981 --> 00:25:05.320
you gotta reinforce that with values set a
context about what the company is

00:25:05.320 --> 00:25:06.443
trying to achieve.

00:25:06.443 --> 00:25:09.739
But the idea to build a system where
amazing amounts of

00:25:09.739 --> 00:25:13.843
service improvement happened In the
marketing, in the content,

00:25:13.843 --> 00:25:18.550
in the technology in the user interface
without direct intervention of me.

00:25:18.550 --> 00:25:22.857
And so some companies operate by, you
know, the product genius at the top and

00:25:22.857 --> 00:25:26.502
we all know, these companies and this
whole motif of, you know,

00:25:26.502 --> 00:25:31.141
to be a great CEO, you've gotta be the
great product person who really, you know,

00:25:31.141 --> 00:25:33.027
and it's intoxicating and fun.

00:25:33.027 --> 00:25:35.999
But you're building incredible amounts of
dependence on yourself.

00:25:35.999 --> 00:25:40.341
And eventually, you know, how will I know
how Turkish consumers consume Netflix?

00:25:40.341 --> 00:25:41.363
It's not that well.

00:25:41.363 --> 00:25:42.100
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; And so

00:25:42.100 --> 00:25:47.365
you're much stronger, obviously building a
distributed set of great thinkers.

00:25:47.365 --> 00:25:48.429
People know that, but

00:25:48.429 --> 00:25:51.567
in the past what they've done is try to
make everything a process.

00:25:51.567 --> 00:25:52.462
They said, okay.

00:25:52.462 --> 00:25:56.408
I don't want it to be a genius, so we're
gonna organize a process for everything.

00:25:56.408 --> 00:25:58.986
And just once in awhile, you heard we're
gonna dummy proof it,

00:25:58.986 --> 00:26:02.062
it's gonna be such a good process that
anybody, that was the idea [UNKNOWN].

00:26:02.062 --> 00:26:06.158
And then if you dummy proof the process,
you only get dummies to work there,

00:26:06.158 --> 00:26:07.762
you know, to do the process.

00:26:07.762 --> 00:26:11.303
And so that's why we're so, you know,
opposed to that.

00:26:11.303 --> 00:26:16.204
Instead so focused on giving people great
freedom, realizing there'll be mistakes.

00:26:16.204 --> 00:26:18.919
But there'll be a lot of positive
achievement too.

00:26:18.919 --> 00:26:20.882
&gt;&gt; So, can you give some examples of that?

00:26:20.882 --> 00:26:23.491
I mean, you know, you talk about it
generically and it sounds great, but

00:26:23.491 --> 00:26:24.725
what are some specific examples?

00:26:24.725 --> 00:26:27.570
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
So I, I, I take pride in making as few

00:26:27.570 --> 00:26:31.376
decisions as possible as opposed to as
many as possible.

00:26:31.376 --> 00:26:34.402
And so when something significant changes
in the content or

00:26:34.402 --> 00:26:37.436
the House of Cards purchase, you know, Ted
explained it.

00:26:37.436 --> 00:26:39.537
It was a huge purchase at that time.

00:26:39.537 --> 00:26:42.998
It was a half an hour conversation, you
know, we went back and forth.

00:26:42.998 --> 00:26:44.657
I said, are you really sure it's a great
bet?

00:26:44.657 --> 00:26:46.003
Yes.
Let's do it.

00:26:46.003 --> 00:26:48.457
You know, it wasn't like me reading the
script and

00:26:48.457 --> 00:26:50.637
me meeting with Kevin Spacey, and you
know?

00:26:50.637 --> 00:26:51.386
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:26:51.386 --> 00:26:54.938
&gt;&gt; or, equivalent examples on the
marketing or

00:26:54.938 --> 00:26:59.203
equivalent examples on the product in the
evolution there.

00:26:59.203 --> 00:27:03.847
So it, it's creating a sense of I, I wanna
make a difference and

00:27:03.847 --> 00:27:05.659
I can make a difference.

00:27:05.659 --> 00:27:08.519
And if that's what the leader gets excited
about is that other

00:27:08.519 --> 00:27:11.545
people are making a difference and you've
got the right people.

00:27:11.545 --> 00:27:13.032
You gotta be super demanding, so

00:27:13.032 --> 00:27:15.643
the, you know, one part is freedom and
responsibility.

00:27:15.643 --> 00:27:17.976
The other part is a high performance
culture.

00:27:17.976 --> 00:27:21.877
Where we say adequate performance, gets a
generous severance package.

00:27:21.877 --> 00:27:26.820
And so, you know, we look at it we wanna
be like a Major League Baseball team and,

00:27:26.820 --> 00:27:30.479
you know, you've only got so many
positions in the field.

00:27:30.479 --> 00:27:35.041
And if everyone is not incredible at what
they do, then you're not gonna win.

00:27:35.041 --> 00:27:36.354
&gt;&gt; So you turn over a lot of people.

00:27:36.354 --> 00:27:37.503
&gt;&gt; And so we turn over a lot of people.

00:27:37.503 --> 00:27:42.059
We feel like, you know, we, we give people
a fair shot and, you know, we,

00:27:42.059 --> 00:27:43.401
we hire pretty openly.

00:27:43.401 --> 00:27:45.060
And they know what they're getting into.

00:27:45.060 --> 00:27:47.724
But we're looking for people who, for whom
freedom and

00:27:47.724 --> 00:27:50.343
responsibility works, cuz they do
incredible work.

00:27:50.343 --> 00:27:51.423
Hm.

00:27:51.423 --> 00:27:55.376
So if you don't make that many decisions
is would be okay if

00:27:55.376 --> 00:27:59.422
they the board paid you on a number of
decisions made basis?

00:27:59.422 --> 00:27:59.933
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:27:59.933 --> 00:28:02.439
[LAUGH].

00:28:02.439 --> 00:28:08.005
&gt;&gt; So the, now, now that you're, you're a
global you know, you're scaling on a,

00:28:08.005 --> 00:28:12.692
on a global basis and you're, you're
moving into content that's did.

00:28:12.692 --> 00:28:15.153
Does your management philosophy change
going forward?

00:28:15.153 --> 00:28:18.856
I mean, is there, is there new thought
that you've had?

00:28:18.856 --> 00:28:22.989
&gt;&gt; No, there's little issues, like we have
thing about no vacation policy and,

00:28:22.989 --> 00:28:25.889
you know, we don't care whether you work
eight hours or

00:28:25.889 --> 00:28:28.435
fourteen hours a day, we care what you get
done.

00:28:28.435 --> 00:28:32.114
And we love someone who's working five
hours a day and gets done a ton.

00:28:32.114 --> 00:28:36.618
But this runs afoul [COUGH] like a French
law and cultural expectations.

00:28:36.618 --> 00:28:39.580
So if you've got employees in France, like
what do you mean there's no vacation?

00:28:39.580 --> 00:28:40.938
You know, it's illegal.

00:28:40.938 --> 00:28:45.300
And so yeah, we're, you know, we're having
to make some

00:28:45.300 --> 00:28:50.910
adjustments to European sensibilities so
there's some things.

00:28:50.910 --> 00:28:55.237
&gt;&gt; So, so let's, let's talk, let's look a
little bit into the future now and

00:28:55.237 --> 00:28:57.447
talk a little bit about, you know?

00:28:57.447 --> 00:29:02.956
Describe to, to us, if you will, what type
of work you're doing to moderni,

00:29:02.956 --> 00:29:08.579
to envision and then moderize the,
modernize the TV viewing experience.

00:29:08.579 --> 00:29:11.078
What is it gonna look like, you know, ten
or

00:29:11.078 --> 00:29:13.931
twenty years to, to this concept of
watching TV?

00:29:13.931 --> 00:29:18.366
&gt;&gt; Well, it's evolving rapidly to be,
it'll all be internet centric.

00:29:18.366 --> 00:29:21.791
So obviously, movies and TV shows will be
purely on demand.

00:29:21.791 --> 00:29:24.693
News is 24 hours, happens all the time.

00:29:24.693 --> 00:29:27.925
You know that'll be on demand.

00:29:27.925 --> 00:29:29.836
And then sports is one of the most
interesting.

00:29:29.836 --> 00:29:32.530
We won't do sports, but other people will.

00:29:32.530 --> 00:29:35.294
And then, you know, there's the whole what
team are you rooting for.

00:29:35.294 --> 00:29:37.173
There's the, all the microsports.

00:29:37.173 --> 00:29:41.687
There's so much that will be on internet
delivery done on a very personalized basis

00:29:41.687 --> 00:29:45.498
of the sport, even though it's, you know,
the game is on at 4 p.m.

00:29:45.498 --> 00:29:47.587
And everybody is watching at the same
time.

00:29:47.587 --> 00:29:51.826
The experience people get can be enhanced
a lot through personalization.

00:29:51.826 --> 00:29:55.682
So I think they'll be a lot of, you know,
great worked on the,

00:29:55.682 --> 00:29:57.188
the internet for sports.

00:29:57.188 --> 00:29:58.747
Obviously, there's watch ESPN and,

00:29:58.747 --> 00:30:00.787
you know, something you wanna carry it
anywhere.

00:30:00.787 --> 00:30:01.728
That's the first step.

00:30:01.728 --> 00:30:02.402
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:30:02.402 --> 00:30:05.603
&gt;&gt; And do you think people will be
watching on TVs or

00:30:05.603 --> 00:30:09.777
do you think they'll be watching on
tablets or PCs or phones?

00:30:09.777 --> 00:30:11.577
&gt;&gt; Who cares?
&gt;&gt; Do you think they'll be watching in

00:30:11.577 --> 00:30:12.274
groups or alone?

00:30:12.274 --> 00:30:16.301
&gt;&gt; I mean, you know, I, again if it's an
Android Tv or an Android tablet, you know.

00:30:16.301 --> 00:30:20.493
So think of the future TV as just a very
large iPad, you know?

00:30:20.493 --> 00:30:23.895
It'll be, you know, two meters big and it
will cost $1000.

00:30:23.895 --> 00:30:26.316
I mean, it's gonna be, you know, the,

00:30:26.316 --> 00:30:30.279
the cost of the semiconductors in screens
is getting less and less.

00:30:30.279 --> 00:30:33.907
And so it'll, you know, it, it, it,
there's not gonna be a TV

00:30:33.907 --> 00:30:37.859
market separate from the phone market and
tablet market and laptop market.

00:30:37.859 --> 00:30:43.816
You know, they'll have similar operating
systems similar interaction patterns.

00:30:43.816 --> 00:30:45.415
You know, a lot of the time,

00:30:45.415 --> 00:30:48.900
you'll use your mobile phone as the remote
control for life.

00:30:48.900 --> 00:30:51.537
You know, your mobile phone will be how
you open your car.

00:30:51.537 --> 00:30:54.519
It'll be, you know, how you control
temperatures in your house.

00:30:54.519 --> 00:30:56.111
Many of you are doing this already.

00:30:56.111 --> 00:30:58.495
But these will be very mainstream things
in 10 or

00:30:58.495 --> 00:31:00.836
20 years and it'll be how you choose
content.

00:31:00.836 --> 00:31:02.205
You know, you'll choose and

00:31:02.205 --> 00:31:04.956
then just flick it to the big screen but
in a lot of ways.

00:31:04.956 --> 00:31:10.368
So you, you won't care this screen size
per se, anymore than you care about,

00:31:10.368 --> 00:31:14.313
you know, a 5 inch iPhone is different
from a 4 inch or-

00:31:14.313 --> 00:31:14.918
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:31:14.918 --> 00:31:16.904
So let's talk about the nature of content.

00:31:16.904 --> 00:31:20.913
So one of the things you said is you think
a lot of the content will be consumed on

00:31:20.913 --> 00:31:22.534
a on demand, on demand basis.

00:31:22.534 --> 00:31:26.662
So what does that mean for, how do you
think things evolve from an ad

00:31:26.662 --> 00:31:31.333
supported model to a subscription model
and talk a little more about that?

00:31:31.333 --> 00:31:32.185
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
I mean,

00:31:32.185 --> 00:31:34.574
we're like HBO in that we're no-ads,

00:31:34.574 --> 00:31:38.859
there'll be other people that are more
like AMC or FX that have ads.

00:31:38.859 --> 00:31:43.378
so, you know, there's a mix of different
business models and preferences and

00:31:43.378 --> 00:31:45.786
everyone will segment a little bit.

00:31:45.786 --> 00:31:50.989
so, you know, YouTube, Hulu, et cetera
will do the, the ad supported part.

00:31:50.989 --> 00:31:55.540
But, you know, the main thing is that,
you, whether it's ad supported or on us,

00:31:55.540 --> 00:31:57.877
it's not a programmer choosing, you know,

00:31:57.877 --> 00:32:00.415
you can watch this show Thursday at 7
o'clock.

00:32:00.415 --> 00:32:01.100
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:32:01.100 --> 00:32:05.186
&gt;&gt; That model, you know, is, is going away
for movies and TV shows.

00:32:05.186 --> 00:32:07.811
&gt;&gt; How about the, how about the cost of
content development?

00:32:07.811 --> 00:32:12.185
You know, in some sense, it's kind of the
golden age for scripted television, but

00:32:12.185 --> 00:32:15.031
the, but the price tags are, are becoming
enormous.

00:32:15.031 --> 00:32:20.349
I mean, I heard today that Gotham was $15
million to do the pilot or,

00:32:20.349 --> 00:32:24.516
or A Game of Thrones is like $12 million
an episode.

00:32:24.516 --> 00:32:29.456
I mean, where, where do you think that
slows down and, and is it, will it

00:32:29.456 --> 00:32:34.431
continue and how do you think about it as
you green light new, new series?

00:32:34.431 --> 00:32:39.221
&gt;&gt; Roughly, what was Ava, what your sense
of Avatar's budget?

00:32:39.221 --> 00:32:41.652
&gt;&gt; 600 million.

00:32:41.652 --> 00:32:43.347
&gt;&gt; Okay.
So by two hours.

00:32:43.347 --> 00:32:45.133
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; So we're, there's so

00:32:45.133 --> 00:32:46.505
much room to go in TV.

00:32:46.505 --> 00:32:47.086
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:32:47.086 --> 00:32:48.328
[LAUGH] Okay.

00:32:48.328 --> 00:32:50.209
So, you know, when, you know,

00:32:50.209 --> 00:32:53.448
the entertainment business is a business
of spectacle.

00:32:53.448 --> 00:32:57.185
And the budget to answer your questions,
will, in TV it will go up and up and up.

00:32:57.185 --> 00:33:00.053
Because there's global distribution,
there's bigger prize.

00:33:00.053 --> 00:33:02.600
There's a bigger, you know, there's only
so

00:33:02.600 --> 00:33:06.113
many brands, some one in Switzerland is
gonna get to watch.

00:33:06.113 --> 00:33:10.049
And to think about, I know about that, you
know, show brand and I want to see it.

00:33:10.049 --> 00:33:13.151
And if you want to be one of those, then
you know,

00:33:13.151 --> 00:33:16.631
having enormity of talent of the set, you
know, the,

00:33:16.631 --> 00:33:21.043
the level of quality that goes in to House
of Cards or Game of Thrones.

00:33:21.043 --> 00:33:22.586
You know, just continue to rise.

00:33:22.586 --> 00:33:23.864
&gt;&gt; Hm.

00:33:23.864 --> 00:33:26.118
And how do you think about Over the Top?

00:33:26.118 --> 00:33:29.481
Are you, do you think about it as
something separate or

00:33:29.481 --> 00:33:33.427
do you, it, it, does it factor into how
Netflix thinks about,

00:33:33.427 --> 00:33:38.421
you know how it delivers its experience or
what kind of content it delivers?

00:33:38.421 --> 00:33:41.019
&gt;&gt; Yeah, we just think about it as
internet.

00:33:41.019 --> 00:33:44.553
You know, so if you're a current TV guy,
you like to feel like you've got

00:33:44.553 --> 00:33:48.192
a fortress and then these bastards are
coming over the top of the wall.

00:33:48.192 --> 00:33:49.304
but, you know, for

00:33:49.304 --> 00:33:53.642
us, it's just creating a great experience
on an internet connected screen.

00:33:53.642 --> 00:33:57.846
Whether that's a phone, a tablet, a
laptop, you know, or

00:33:57.846 --> 00:34:00.050
a, a television screen.

00:34:00.050 --> 00:34:03.200
&gt;&gt; so, as you look into the future, who,

00:34:03.200 --> 00:34:06.842
who do you consider are your, your
friends?

00:34:06.842 --> 00:34:11.014
And who are the people who are maybe less
then friends?

00:34:11.014 --> 00:34:14.230
&gt;&gt; Well let's see the interesting part is
less then friends.

00:34:14.230 --> 00:34:20.094
So we have supplier type battles with ISPs
Comcast, et cetera.

00:34:20.094 --> 00:34:22.746
Because they say, we should help pay for
the network and

00:34:22.746 --> 00:34:25.338
we say, well why don't you pay for our
content?

00:34:25.338 --> 00:34:29.886
And then like that and so they're used to
getting paid and we don't want to pay.

00:34:29.886 --> 00:34:34.082
So, you know, it's a classic supplier
relationship dynamic.

00:34:34.082 --> 00:34:39.963
Then with c, other distributors like FX or
HBO we compete.

00:34:39.963 --> 00:34:41.654
But we like each other a lot and

00:34:41.654 --> 00:34:44.979
then like, I subscribe to HBO, cuz I like
watching VEEP.

00:34:44.979 --> 00:34:48.148
And so what we realized is if they have
great shows,

00:34:48.148 --> 00:34:50.146
people with subscribe to both.

00:34:50.146 --> 00:34:52.947
And really we don't compete against each
other so

00:34:52.947 --> 00:34:55.626
much, we all compete for entertainment
time.

00:34:55.626 --> 00:34:58.145
So, think what you do on a Wednesday
night.

00:34:58.145 --> 00:35:01.418
You go home and, you know, what do you do
to relax?

00:35:01.418 --> 00:35:05.226
You know, you can drink a bottle of wine
or you could watch Netflix or

00:35:05.226 --> 00:35:08.409
you could watch a DVD or you could watch
something on iTunes or

00:35:08.409 --> 00:35:12.238
watch something on cable or watch
something on Hulu or play a board game.

00:35:12.238 --> 00:35:14.642
You know, or like a hundred other things.

00:35:14.642 --> 00:35:16.720
So all of that is entertainment time.

00:35:16.720 --> 00:35:19.460
We compete for some little bit of that.

00:35:19.460 --> 00:35:22.582
Enough to justify, you know, 9 bucks a
month.

00:35:22.582 --> 00:35:27.208
So we don't notice it when competitors do
something and the reason is we

00:35:27.208 --> 00:35:31.692
don't notice it in terms of our growth,
because we're all competing for

00:35:31.692 --> 00:35:36.282
this huge pool of entertainment time and a
huge pool of disposable income.

00:35:36.282 --> 00:35:38.154
There's no separate market for

00:35:38.154 --> 00:35:41.563
the share of internet delivered video
compared to cable.

00:35:41.563 --> 00:35:43.143
I mean, the customer doesn't care.

00:35:43.143 --> 00:35:46.624
They're looking relaxation, entertainment
and video gaming.

00:35:46.624 --> 00:35:51.128
I mean, we compete actively, you know,
with that in the loose sense of

00:35:51.128 --> 00:35:55.877
a substitution for unwind time and or in
that case, freedom from boredom.

00:35:55.877 --> 00:35:59.256
&gt;&gt; Does that mean that maybe you go into
other media types?

00:35:59.256 --> 00:36:03.979
&gt;&gt; We just, so far movies and TV shows,
which is a lean back, enjo,

00:36:03.979 --> 00:36:08.241
get wrapped up in the story experience has
been our sweet spot.

00:36:08.241 --> 00:36:12.046
And when we think about expanding beyond
it, we're like, wow, movies and

00:36:12.046 --> 00:36:13.002
TV shows is so big.

00:36:13.002 --> 00:36:15.643
I mean, on a global basis, there should
be, you know,

00:36:15.643 --> 00:36:19.595
a couple billion people who are interested
in that and we're only at 50 million.

00:36:19.595 --> 00:36:22.382
And we have a fraction of all shows and,
and movies.

00:36:22.382 --> 00:36:27.537
So we're much better off just staying in
what we do and making it much much bigger.

00:36:27.537 --> 00:36:31.124
And getting to many more shows, many
members around the world.

00:36:31.124 --> 00:36:34.271
And if we're only the movie and TV show
brand in 20 years and

00:36:34.271 --> 00:36:37.294
we're not the sports brand, we're not
something else,

00:36:37.294 --> 00:36:41.074
that's still on a global basis, that's a
phenomenally large market.

00:36:41.074 --> 00:36:43.582
&gt;&gt; Would you ever get into sports?

00:36:43.582 --> 00:36:45.380
&gt;&gt; I don't think so.

00:36:45.380 --> 00:36:48.236
Too expensive, too hard no differential
confidence.

00:36:48.236 --> 00:36:48.759
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:36:48.759 --> 00:36:50.102
Okay.

00:36:50.102 --> 00:36:55.249
who, as you look at the, the, the future
and you, the, do you think that,

00:36:55.249 --> 00:37:00.961
that what, what are, are, are the, is, is
the cable bundle gonna continue or

00:37:00.961 --> 00:37:05.949
do you see increasing disaggregation the
way you're starting to see it,

00:37:05.949 --> 00:37:07.821
you know, happening now?

00:37:07.821 --> 00:37:11.896
&gt;&gt; The likely case is that it's mostly
stays aggregated and drifts.

00:37:11.896 --> 00:37:14.930
It's about a 100 million in the US, it's
been a 100 million for

00:37:14.930 --> 00:37:15.913
the last three years.

00:37:15.913 --> 00:37:21.219
And then it goes, you know, 98 and a half,
97, you know?

00:37:21.219 --> 00:37:24.597
[COUGH] Ticks down, if you think of
broadcast like ABC.

00:37:24.597 --> 00:37:27.604
They've gone down 2 or 3% points every
year for 30 years and

00:37:27.604 --> 00:37:31.122
they've managed the cost and it's still a
great business economically.

00:37:31.122 --> 00:37:34.315
It's less impactful on society, but it's a
great business.

00:37:34.315 --> 00:37:36.581
So think of that as the likely case as
more and

00:37:36.581 --> 00:37:39.480
more young people in particular live an
internet life.

00:37:39.480 --> 00:37:41.071
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:37:41.071 --> 00:37:43.446
So on a personal basis who do you,

00:37:43.446 --> 00:37:48.274
you know, there are a lot of people who
look up to you and everybody says well,

00:37:48.274 --> 00:37:52.540
we're the Netflix of x, the Netflix of y,
and who do you look up to?

00:37:52.540 --> 00:37:56.535
I mean, as you were kind of coming up, who
were your person heroes?

00:37:56.535 --> 00:37:58.105
&gt;&gt; Well, interestingly,

00:37:58.105 --> 00:38:03.571
in the you mentioned in the startup Cohera
[UNKNOWN] that I worked at when I was 28.

00:38:03.571 --> 00:38:05.712
And I worked so hard as an engineer
writing code,

00:38:05.712 --> 00:38:08.074
you know, there every night all night kind
of thing.

00:38:08.074 --> 00:38:09.529
How hard can you push?

00:38:09.529 --> 00:38:10.318
You know,

00:38:10.318 --> 00:38:15.960
I would build up over time on my desk this
kind of gross set of coffee cups.

00:38:15.960 --> 00:38:20.079
And then you know, now and then, the
janitor would clean them all.

00:38:20.079 --> 00:38:22.776
And I learned that if I just waited long
enough, I didn't have to clean them,

00:38:22.776 --> 00:38:24.119
you know, the janitor would do that.

00:38:24.119 --> 00:38:27.648
[LAUGH] And one morning, I came in really
early to work,

00:38:27.648 --> 00:38:31.867
you know, five in the morning and I walk
into the bathroom and

00:38:31.867 --> 00:38:36.858
there are all my cups being cleaned and I
looked up and it was our CEO.

00:38:36.858 --> 00:38:38.979
Barry Clothman was cleaning them.

00:38:38.979 --> 00:38:41.196
And so, you know, it's early in the
morning.

00:38:41.196 --> 00:38:43.740
I'm like, I'm trying to understand the
situation,

00:38:43.740 --> 00:38:45.410
cuz I see him there scrubbing away.

00:38:45.410 --> 00:38:49.289
[LAUGH] So finally, I just asked him, I
was like Barry, what are you doing?

00:38:49.289 --> 00:38:51.134
And he goes, I'm cleaning a cup.

00:38:51.134 --> 00:38:53.688
And I was like, have you been doing it the
whole years?

00:38:53.688 --> 00:38:54.791
Yeah.

00:38:54.791 --> 00:38:56.613
And I'm like and you never said anything?

00:38:56.613 --> 00:38:57.228
No.

00:38:57.228 --> 00:38:58.494
Why?

00:38:58.494 --> 00:39:02.697
And he said, well you work so hard and
this is the only thing I can do for you.

00:39:02.697 --> 00:39:03.254
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:39:03.254 --> 00:39:06.548
&gt;&gt; And I just thought, wow, I will follow
this guy to the end of the earth and

00:39:06.548 --> 00:39:08.072
that is exactly where he lead us.

00:39:08.072 --> 00:39:13.313
[LAUGH] So what happened is he was an
incredibly charismatic guy

00:39:13.313 --> 00:39:17.767
that didn't have a good market product fit
vision.

00:39:17.767 --> 00:39:20.914
And we built an incredibly elaborate
product and

00:39:20.914 --> 00:39:25.916
we ultimately sold one copy of it to one
customer and they never installed it.

00:39:25.916 --> 00:39:28.934
So, you know, it's like, it's a funny
thing about leadership.

00:39:28.934 --> 00:39:31.686
You can be very personally compelling and
high integrity, that's great.

00:39:31.686 --> 00:39:34.065
But you also gotta lead people in the
right direction.

00:39:34.065 --> 00:39:36.446
Not into the box canyon.

00:39:36.446 --> 00:39:37.965
&gt;&gt; That's great.

00:39:37.965 --> 00:39:42.284
I'm gonna take questions from the audience
in, in a few minutes, so

00:39:42.284 --> 00:39:44.871
please please think of some.

00:39:44.871 --> 00:39:47.597
So what advice do you have for young CEOs?

00:39:47.597 --> 00:39:51.526
Just generally speaking about you started
many companies?

00:39:51.526 --> 00:39:54.487
Just generally speaking, you know, what
advice would you have?

00:39:54.487 --> 00:39:58.286
And then in particular, talk a little bit
about building culture and

00:39:58.286 --> 00:40:01.381
values and when is the best time to
articulate it.

00:40:01.381 --> 00:40:06.810
&gt;&gt; I would say, memorize the first 86
pages of Jim Collins's first big book,

00:40:06.810 --> 00:40:09.075
Beyond Entrepreneurship.

00:40:09.075 --> 00:40:12.794
Someone gave it to me in 94 and I've read
it every year since.

00:40:12.794 --> 00:40:17.417
And the first 86 pages are absolutely
incredible.

00:40:17.417 --> 00:40:21.014
And so that would be their, their very
practical life.

00:40:21.014 --> 00:40:24.765
&gt;&gt; Well, and, and then talk a little bit
about building culture and values.

00:40:24.765 --> 00:40:28.525
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think everyone tries to build
culture and values at first, you know?

00:40:28.525 --> 00:40:31.980
You have to stage it with a company to the
degree that you are 20 people and

00:40:31.980 --> 00:40:33.169
you've got no revenue.

00:40:33.169 --> 00:40:36.315
It's sort of a very implicit culture and,
you know, you spend time on,

00:40:36.315 --> 00:40:38.033
you know, things that could kill you.

00:40:38.033 --> 00:40:41.227
Like, you know, product to market fit and
that's appropriate.

00:40:41.227 --> 00:40:45.047
And then, you know, later if you're going
to last, then you say, okay.

00:40:45.047 --> 00:40:49.283
How do we, you know, make sure that as new
people come in the culture gets better.

00:40:49.283 --> 00:40:53.297
And one of the big things is probably this
idea that you get better as

00:40:53.297 --> 00:40:54.310
you get bigger.

00:40:54.310 --> 00:40:56.941
So everyone implicitly has the idea that,
you know,

00:40:56.941 --> 00:41:00.811
you start sucking as you get bigger more
political, harder to get stuff done.

00:41:00.811 --> 00:41:04.074
And you have to actively fight acceptance
of that to come up

00:41:04.074 --> 00:41:06.138
with very concrete examples where,

00:41:06.138 --> 00:41:10.622
like Netflix is significantly bettering
culture than three to five years ago.

00:41:10.622 --> 00:41:12.629
And then ten years ago and 15.

00:41:12.629 --> 00:41:16.229
And why is, cuz we got more brains
thinking about the problem.

00:41:16.229 --> 00:41:18.108
This is where Malthus really went wrong.

00:41:18.108 --> 00:41:21.938
So of course, Malthus, in the late 1700s
said, everyone's gonna starve,

00:41:21.938 --> 00:41:25.446
cuz you look at all the people growth and
you look at the fixed agriculture.

00:41:25.446 --> 00:41:29.241
And he didn't realize that, you know,
basically as the people grew,

00:41:29.241 --> 00:41:32.168
the ideas to improve agriculture would
also improve.

00:41:32.168 --> 00:41:35.408
And so we've had a massive explosion in
agricultural productivity and

00:41:35.408 --> 00:41:38.543
the ability to feed 7 billion people,
which he would be shocked by.

00:41:38.543 --> 00:41:39.286
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:41:39.286 --> 00:41:42.690
&gt;&gt; And it's the same thing in company
culture, which is if you have more,

00:41:42.690 --> 00:41:46.608
you know, a thousand really thoughtful
people, thinking about how to improve,

00:41:46.608 --> 00:41:49.200
you make more progress than if you've got
a hundred.

00:41:49.200 --> 00:41:52.809
And so we're actually getting better as we
get bigger.

00:41:52.809 --> 00:41:55.382
But it's constantly changing the frame of
reference,

00:41:55.382 --> 00:41:57.401
that's really what the leader does.

00:41:57.401 --> 00:42:01.824
You know, that this is possible and that
in fact, we should aspire to it and, and

00:42:01.824 --> 00:42:02.747
make it happen.

00:42:02.747 --> 00:42:04.415
&gt;&gt; That's great.

00:42:04.415 --> 00:42:08.579
I don't know if there's a microphone or
going around, but

00:42:08.579 --> 00:42:12.662
let me ask you one last question, which is
if, if you are,

00:42:12.662 --> 00:42:16.539
we know you're going to be at Netflix for
a lot longer.

00:42:16.539 --> 00:42:21.544
But if you were starting a new company,
what area would you look at, right now?

00:42:21.544 --> 00:42:22.299
&gt;&gt; Well, that's interesting.

00:42:22.299 --> 00:42:28.282
Well you know, if I were starting a new
company I'd probably try something in bio.

00:42:28.282 --> 00:42:32.914
Because it's a totally different area and
it's, you know, there's, you know,

00:42:32.914 --> 00:42:37.239
the genetic revolution, you know, for the
next, you know, hundred years.

00:42:37.239 --> 00:42:39.039
Thinking, you know, will be incredible.

00:42:39.039 --> 00:42:41.537
And sometime in that time period, you
know,

00:42:41.537 --> 00:42:44.645
there'll be various kinds of post-human
life forms.

00:42:44.645 --> 00:42:49.186
And, you know, we humans are very ego and,
and species-centric.

00:42:49.186 --> 00:42:52.808
But, you know, it may turn out that what
gets done and, and

00:42:52.808 --> 00:42:57.382
the races between, you know, alternative
life and augmented humanity.

00:42:57.382 --> 00:42:59.589
And then when we could manipulate
genetics, so

00:42:59.589 --> 00:43:01.307
that you never get a cold, you know?

00:43:01.307 --> 00:43:03.400
It's gonna be an incredible world.

00:43:03.400 --> 00:43:07.227
So there's, there's so much going on in,
in that world.

00:43:07.227 --> 00:43:11.639
But, you know, I would say a, anything
that people are passionate about.

00:43:11.639 --> 00:43:16.096
Because then each area, it's just real
exciting when you get into it.

00:43:16.096 --> 00:43:16.665
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:43:16.665 --> 00:43:18.146
Well, thank you.

00:43:18.146 --> 00:43:23.840
I, Reid's graciously offered to take some
questions, so please.

00:43:23.840 --> 00:43:25.686
Yeah, sir.

00:43:25.686 --> 00:43:30.980
&gt;&gt; Yes, I'd to ask [INAUDIBLE].

00:43:30.980 --> 00:43:32.501
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:43:32.501 --> 00:43:34.800
&gt;&gt; Well, any business can be disrupted.

00:43:34.800 --> 00:43:39.506
If you look at Kodak, you know, it was
film images for a 100 years and

00:43:39.506 --> 00:43:43.600
then came the digital revolution and, and
knocked them off.

00:43:43.600 --> 00:43:47.649
And so you can be, you know, Kodak think
of this, such a shame because you've got

00:43:47.649 --> 00:43:50.937
a brand there that represents, you know,
family and memories and

00:43:50.937 --> 00:43:54.626
the warmth of that imagery up till 20
years ago it's been nominal.

00:43:54.626 --> 00:43:58.065
So when we think about, you know, what's
our Kodak moment.

00:43:58.065 --> 00:44:01.449
So there's one threat, which is streaming
goes away,

00:44:01.449 --> 00:44:04.334
because there's some subatomic innovation.

00:44:04.334 --> 00:44:06.640
Suddenly, you've got all content in the,
you know, and

00:44:06.640 --> 00:44:08.015
no one does the internet anymore.

00:44:08.015 --> 00:44:09.953
Its a post-internet world.

00:44:09.953 --> 00:44:13.072
That seems not in the next 50 or 100
years.

00:44:13.072 --> 00:44:16.490
Then the more material threat is, is that
movies and

00:44:16.490 --> 00:44:18.753
TV shows become passe in society.

00:44:18.753 --> 00:44:22.323
Kind of like novels are today, you know,
you read one a year,

00:44:22.323 --> 00:44:26.717
it's a very small business, you know, and
then things like, Oculus and,

00:44:26.717 --> 00:44:31.594
you know, other scenarios are incredibly
exciting and the culture's just moved on

00:44:31.594 --> 00:44:35.400
from what our specialty is of delivering
our emotional [UNKNOWN].

00:44:35.400 --> 00:44:38.653
So that's the one that could happen over
ten or twenty years.

00:44:38.653 --> 00:44:40.990
But on the other hand, if we look at the
movie and

00:44:40.990 --> 00:44:43.912
TV show market, you know, it's been a
hundred year run and

00:44:43.912 --> 00:44:47.672
its done very well despite the rise of
video gaming over the last 20 years.

00:44:47.672 --> 00:44:48.670
It hasn't shrunk.

00:44:48.670 --> 00:44:53.288
So it seems to have real legs of human
story telling like that, you know,

00:44:53.288 --> 00:44:57.387
around the fire time and it's the modern
replacement for that.

00:44:57.387 --> 00:45:01.904
So those would be the, the two big ones
that we keep an eye on.

00:45:01.904 --> 00:45:04.423
[NOISE] Thank you.

00:45:04.423 --> 00:45:08.060
Thank you for the, the wonderful talk.

00:45:08.060 --> 00:45:12.800
The question I have is, is from about five
years ago when Netflix was moving from

00:45:12.800 --> 00:45:18.690
the DVD delivery model into the internet,
based streaming model.

00:45:18.690 --> 00:45:23.459
[NOISE] Came up with, two plan, scheme
where you had,

00:45:23.459 --> 00:45:26.573
899 for the internet and DVD and 799.

00:45:26.573 --> 00:45:27.633
&gt;&gt; Extra episode.

00:45:27.633 --> 00:45:28.259
&gt;&gt; Exactly.

00:45:28.259 --> 00:45:29.464
Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Just view to the point.

00:45:29.464 --> 00:45:29.964
[NOISE].
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:45:29.964 --> 00:45:30.489
&gt;&gt; [COUGH].

00:45:30.489 --> 00:45:31.019
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK] I

00:45:31.019 --> 00:45:32.347
was one of the shareholders.

00:45:32.347 --> 00:45:33.620
I lost-

00:45:33.620 --> 00:45:36.210
Yeah.
A lot of value during that time.

00:45:36.210 --> 00:45:37.820
Want to understand what was happening.

00:45:37.820 --> 00:45:38.745
And I know
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:45:38.745 --> 00:45:40.372
&gt;&gt; Netflix got a lot of backlash at that
time.

00:45:40.372 --> 00:45:41.640
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
It did.

00:45:41.640 --> 00:45:43.170
And deservedly so in hindsight.

00:45:43.170 --> 00:45:47.080
But we were so obsessed with not being
Kodak.

00:45:47.080 --> 00:45:48.918
You know?
With not being AOL.

00:45:48.918 --> 00:45:50.780
With not being the company that, you know,

00:45:50.780 --> 00:45:56.150
clung to the last dollar from its roots
that they missed the big thing.

00:45:56.150 --> 00:45:57.355
And so, we said, you know?

00:45:57.355 --> 00:46:01.330
We looked at, I mean, even Blockbuster was
run by very sharp people.

00:46:01.330 --> 00:46:06.420
And so, you know, very smart managers
stick to the current models too much.

00:46:06.420 --> 00:46:07.585
And we said, look, if,

00:46:07.585 --> 00:46:11.151
if there's a bias, we have to be more
aggressive than we're comfortable.

00:46:11.151 --> 00:46:14.260
Cuz smart people, which they had been,
make this mistake.

00:46:14.260 --> 00:46:18.610
So we would have a phrase that we have to
be so aggressive it makes our skin crawl.

00:46:18.610 --> 00:46:23.160
And so then when the idea of splitting
came up, it made our skin crawl.

00:46:23.160 --> 00:46:24.093
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But we said well,

00:46:24.093 --> 00:46:25.632
that's what we had to be preparing for.

00:46:25.632 --> 00:46:26.134
You know?

00:46:26.134 --> 00:46:27.950
We're the company that's gonna be hyper
aggressive.

00:46:28.952 --> 00:46:31.969
And in a perfect hindsight the main thing
we did wrong is

00:46:31.969 --> 00:46:34.440
not grandfathering the pricing.

00:46:34.440 --> 00:46:37.500
So if we had grandfathered so it wasn't an
effective price increase, it,

00:46:37.500 --> 00:46:38.370
it would've been fine.

00:46:38.370 --> 00:46:39.160
But we missed that.

00:46:39.160 --> 00:46:40.570
We didn't do that.

00:46:41.670 --> 00:46:45.520
And so there was a huge uproar because we
took 20 million US households in

00:46:45.520 --> 00:46:48.830
the middle of the 2008 reception,
recession, and

00:46:48.830 --> 00:46:51.500
gave them a 60% price increase.

00:46:51.500 --> 00:46:54.740
So then the mob effect and crowd effect
kicked in.

00:46:54.740 --> 00:46:58.060
And we, and we went from loved to hated
very quickly.

00:46:58.060 --> 00:46:59.570
And so, you know?

00:46:59.570 --> 00:47:00.790
In hindsight, it's kind of obvious.

00:47:00.790 --> 00:47:02.100
Look.
Should I give 20 million people in

00:47:02.100 --> 00:47:05.350
the middle of a recession a 60% price
increase.

00:47:05.350 --> 00:47:06.016
You know?
No.

00:47:06.016 --> 00:47:08.850
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [COUGH] But, you know?

00:47:08.850 --> 00:47:12.560
We were blinded by this desire to not get
trapped with DVD.

00:47:12.560 --> 00:47:15.580
So, we got through it barely but,.

00:47:15.580 --> 00:47:17.410
And, of course, now DVD is pretty
separate.

00:47:17.410 --> 00:47:20.390
DVD's about 6 million members, and they
love the service.

00:47:20.390 --> 00:47:21.905
And it's got great selection.

00:47:21.905 --> 00:47:23.419
DVD's got some real advantages.

00:47:24.420 --> 00:47:28.500
And then now we've got 35 million US on
streaming and

00:47:28.500 --> 00:47:29.850
about 15 million international.

00:47:29.850 --> 00:47:33.047
&gt;&gt; That's great.

00:47:33.047 --> 00:47:34.830
yeah.

00:47:34.830 --> 00:47:38.380
&gt;&gt; Reed.
One of my friends visiting from Beijing,

00:47:38.380 --> 00:47:42.610
China, says that House of Cards is, is
wildly popular in the capital there.

00:47:43.660 --> 00:47:47.545
And, China's becoming the second largest
movie market in the world.

00:47:47.545 --> 00:47:54.510
Could you talk a little about your, China
strategy?

00:47:54.510 --> 00:47:58.300
&gt;&gt; We're not sure, you know, if Netflix
will be able to be successful in China.

00:47:58.300 --> 00:47:59.550
We're trying to study the issue.

00:48:01.140 --> 00:48:02.840
The interesting one is House of Cards.

00:48:02.840 --> 00:48:04.620
Which hopefully many of you have seen.

00:48:04.620 --> 00:48:06.390
And it's got a Chinese storyline so

00:48:06.390 --> 00:48:09.880
you think, oh, it doesn't make them look
that good.

00:48:09.880 --> 00:48:11.390
And so why's it so successful?

00:48:12.610 --> 00:48:15.505
It's because in China, this is seen as a
documentary.

00:48:15.505 --> 00:48:18.050
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:48:18.050 --> 00:48:22.930
&gt;&gt; And they think that our guys kill
people and, and become president.

00:48:22.930 --> 00:48:24.275
Because that's their reality.

00:48:24.275 --> 00:48:25.480
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:48:25.480 --> 00:48:27.230
&gt;&gt; And so, you know?

00:48:27.230 --> 00:48:30.680
It, the thing you see in Chinese people
about watching House of Cards is, yeah,

00:48:30.680 --> 00:48:31.240
you're no better.

00:48:31.240 --> 00:48:33.320
You see your people rise, and they think
it's all true.

00:48:35.200 --> 00:48:36.280
So, you know?

00:48:36.280 --> 00:48:39.570
You get to see what you want in, in
different shows.

00:48:39.570 --> 00:48:42.540
So we have a lot to learn to really
understand, China.

00:48:42.540 --> 00:48:43.040
That shows.

00:48:46.070 --> 00:48:48.920
&gt;&gt; Yeah, please.

00:48:48.920 --> 00:48:51.080
&gt;&gt; Well, congratulations on your award.

00:48:51.080 --> 00:48:55.370
I'm representing an organization called
Stanford Women on Boards, which is,

00:48:55.370 --> 00:48:59.710
as the name says, women, across the
university who are on for-profit and,

00:48:59.710 --> 00:49:00.830
and, not-for-profit boards.

00:49:02.240 --> 00:49:04.850
You've certainly had great success with
gender diversity.

00:49:04.850 --> 00:49:09.390
And what advice would you give both to the
women who are on boards seeking other

00:49:09.390 --> 00:49:13.440
boards or organizations who are looking
for great candidates for boards?

00:49:14.500 --> 00:49:16.910
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
It's a super tough issue both on race and

00:49:16.910 --> 00:49:17.960
gender.

00:49:17.960 --> 00:49:19.870
Because the people that get recruited for

00:49:19.870 --> 00:49:22.950
boards are people you're really
comfortable with.

00:49:22.950 --> 00:49:27.010
And depending on your, social circle and
others, then you

00:49:27.010 --> 00:49:31.900
don't have as many African American or
Hispanic or women executive roles.

00:49:31.900 --> 00:49:34.644
And getting the references is really hard.

00:49:34.644 --> 00:49:38.540
You know,I've been [COUGH] really.

00:49:38.540 --> 00:49:42.095
I, I think I started you know, twenty
years ago as a classic Silicon Valley.

00:49:42.095 --> 00:49:43.540
It's a meritocracy.

00:49:43.540 --> 00:49:44.710
It'll change.

00:49:44.710 --> 00:49:47.840
And I realize now that we need to make,
you know,

00:49:47.840 --> 00:49:51.350
really active efforts on building the
connection that works.

00:49:51.350 --> 00:49:54.910
Both in that case for, for in your case,
for female executives but, you know?

00:49:54.910 --> 00:49:56.780
It's broader, obviously, than just gender.

00:49:58.210 --> 00:50:01.930
Because it's a very intimate relationship
with the board.

00:50:01.930 --> 00:50:05.530
And so you bring people in, that you
really, really trust.

00:50:05.530 --> 00:50:08.750
And that takes an amount of time and
comfort and exposure.

00:50:08.750 --> 00:50:10.630
And so, it's a, it's a deep problem,

00:50:10.630 --> 00:50:15.330
which we'll make some progress on, with,
expanding contact networks.

00:50:16.450 --> 00:50:22.410
Interesting is why I just joined the
Hispanic Foundation of Silicon Valley,

00:50:22.410 --> 00:50:24.190
which is a great organization.

00:50:24.190 --> 00:50:27.070
But, you know, what do I really know about
the challenges for

00:50:27.070 --> 00:50:28.690
Hispanic, people in America?

00:50:28.690 --> 00:50:29.860
Not a lot.

00:50:29.860 --> 00:50:30.914
But I'll be a donor.

00:50:30.914 --> 00:50:33.051
And I'll really get to know, [COUGH]
excuse me.

00:50:33.051 --> 00:50:34.636
The board and the other members and

00:50:34.636 --> 00:50:38.405
make an active effort to say, oh, that's a
person that could be an executive, or

00:50:38.405 --> 00:50:42.760
a board member, or something like that, or
for my organization or for some other.

00:50:42.760 --> 00:50:47.480
And, you know, we all need to make as much
effort as possible on that, you know,

00:50:47.480 --> 00:50:51.513
cross, you know, sector, integration.

00:50:52.620 --> 00:50:55.060
So check back with me in two years, and
I'll see if we've, you know,

00:50:55.060 --> 00:50:59.556
if we're as successful on the Latino side
as we've been on the, the gender side.

00:50:59.556 --> 00:51:01.145
&gt;&gt; yeah.

00:51:01.145 --> 00:51:03.130
Sorry.

00:51:03.130 --> 00:51:04.530
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much for the talk.

00:51:04.530 --> 00:51:07.720
I have been super interested about your
culture.

00:51:07.720 --> 00:51:11.000
So I wanna ask a question in, in that
regard.

00:51:11.000 --> 00:51:14.630
You talked about hire quickly and openly.

00:51:14.630 --> 00:51:17.510
You also talked about letting go pretty
quickly.

00:51:17.510 --> 00:51:20.460
So, how do you make sure during this very
short time frame,

00:51:20.460 --> 00:51:24.360
you ensure there's enough diversity,
enough different ideas,

00:51:24.360 --> 00:51:27.400
so that not everybody inside looks just
the same?

00:51:30.300 --> 00:51:31.550
yeah.
I don't think the,

00:51:31.550 --> 00:51:33.920
we don't let people go quickly.

00:51:33.920 --> 00:51:36.780
we, in other words, it's not like oh, one
day you wake up.

00:51:36.780 --> 00:51:38.810
So, you know, we try to think through.

00:51:38.810 --> 00:51:42.070
Just as you would do on a professional
sports team is, you know?

00:51:42.070 --> 00:51:43.490
You have one bad game.

00:51:43.490 --> 00:51:44.370
You know?

00:51:44.370 --> 00:51:46.050
You're not out.

00:51:46.050 --> 00:51:49.310
It's a, it's a track record you build up
over time.

00:51:49.310 --> 00:51:53.275
And then, you know, if your leadership,
your manager or their manager and

00:51:53.275 --> 00:51:56.710
HR think, you know, you're unlikely to
develop into the great player we need in

00:51:56.710 --> 00:51:58.980
that position, then you do get the
package.

00:51:58.980 --> 00:52:00.610
That's the adequate performance.

00:52:02.170 --> 00:52:02.710
and, you know?

00:52:02.710 --> 00:52:04.800
We try to select and we.

00:52:04.800 --> 00:52:05.630
To answer your question,

00:52:05.630 --> 00:52:09.180
which is how do you not weed out,
essentially, diverse ideas,.

00:52:09.180 --> 00:52:13.130
The answer is we reward diverse ideas that
often,

00:52:14.810 --> 00:52:20.420
prizing people who argue with me or with
others, you know, to make it safe to do.

00:52:20.420 --> 00:52:22.440
And that, I don't think anyone would say,

00:52:22.440 --> 00:52:25.490
oh if you disagree with senior management
that's what gets you out.

00:52:27.280 --> 00:52:30.310
You know, we, we need to live that in
practice to make sure that

00:52:30.310 --> 00:52:33.330
people feel safe if they're good ideas.

00:52:33.330 --> 00:52:36.000
It doesn't mean that every idea's a good
idea.

00:52:36.000 --> 00:52:40.405
And to the degree that you
confrontationally brought up, you know?

00:52:40.405 --> 00:52:43.475
Twenty crazy ideas in a row, you know,
over six months?

00:52:43.475 --> 00:52:46.989
We probably would give you a package
because it's, that, like,

00:52:46.989 --> 00:52:49.170
not a great track record.

00:52:49.170 --> 00:52:51.420
But again it's not an instantaneous thing.

00:52:51.420 --> 00:52:53.120
It's over a track record.

00:52:56.930 --> 00:52:57.830
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:52:57.830 --> 00:52:58.670
Sorry.
Where's the mic?

00:53:03.660 --> 00:53:10.962
Nora?
[NOISE].

00:53:10.962 --> 00:53:13.790
&gt;&gt; Congratulations on your fantastic
success.

00:53:13.790 --> 00:53:17.380
Could you talk a bit about work/life
balance, and how you make all that make-

00:53:17.380 --> 00:53:18.081
&gt;&gt; My what?
Sorry.

00:53:18.081 --> 00:53:19.672
&gt;&gt; Work and life balance.

00:53:19.672 --> 00:53:22.440
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
Work/life balance is the wrong metaphor.

00:53:23.480 --> 00:53:25.510
Its really work/life integration.

00:53:26.900 --> 00:53:30.410
You know?
So the reality is, you know,

00:53:30.410 --> 00:53:33.400
we come up with great ideas in the shower.

00:53:33.400 --> 00:53:34.920
You know?
That's not work time.

00:53:34.920 --> 00:53:35.460
You know?

00:53:35.460 --> 00:53:37.400
But it is really.

00:53:37.400 --> 00:53:40.510
And so I think each individual has to
figure out in their situation.

00:53:40.510 --> 00:53:41.520
How do they stay healthy?

00:53:41.520 --> 00:53:44.130
How do they have healthy relationships?

00:53:44.130 --> 00:53:46.460
And we're super flexible on it.

00:53:46.460 --> 00:53:47.820
We don't, we tend to use,

00:53:47.820 --> 00:53:52.070
pretty strict about meetings, being just
during the nine to five day.

00:53:52.070 --> 00:53:55.610
And we think of office time as time to
have physical in person meetings.

00:53:56.740 --> 00:54:00.860
And then beyond that it's sort of how you
run your schedule is up to you,

00:54:00.860 --> 00:54:02.510
back to the freedom and responsibility.

00:54:03.800 --> 00:54:07.610
And ultimately we care, again, what you
get accomplished.

00:54:07.610 --> 00:54:10.330
Not, like, do you also coach your kids'
baseball team.

00:54:10.330 --> 00:54:10.899
Which is fine.

00:54:12.410 --> 00:54:14.410
And so there's not,.

00:54:14.410 --> 00:54:18.000
Because it's much more about the work as
opposed to what hours you put in,

00:54:18.000 --> 00:54:20.140
it doesn't tend to be.

00:54:20.140 --> 00:54:21.138
You know?

00:54:21.138 --> 00:54:24.045
That I gotta show up at nine even though
I'm not getting much done at

00:54:24.045 --> 00:54:26.430
night because everyone else is there and I
need to be seen.

00:54:26.430 --> 00:54:29.030
Like a kind of investment banking type
hold scenario.

00:54:30.990 --> 00:54:31.760
&gt;&gt; Sir.

00:54:31.760 --> 00:54:33.760
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much.

00:54:33.760 --> 00:54:37.960
You mentioned the, leverage that you have
from the data that you collect.

00:54:37.960 --> 00:54:42.800
Can you talk about, a bit more about, what
kind of data, you collect?

00:54:42.800 --> 00:54:47.800
And what insights you derive from that and
use it in the content that you create?

00:54:48.820 --> 00:54:51.330
&gt;&gt; Well, we're always trying to predict,
what are you going to enjoy?

00:54:51.330 --> 00:54:54.130
That's the, if you watch a bunch of
Netflix,

00:54:54.130 --> 00:54:56.590
you're gonna retain and evangelize.

00:54:56.590 --> 00:54:58.840
So if you watch three hours, you've got a
high,

00:54:58.840 --> 00:55:00.930
a month, you've got a high churn rate.

00:55:00.930 --> 00:55:03.660
If you watch 30 hours, it's a median churn
rate.

00:55:03.660 --> 00:55:07.160
If you watch 100 hours on Netflix a month,
you know, you love it.

00:55:07.160 --> 00:55:08.040
You rave about the service.

00:55:08.040 --> 00:55:11.670
So think of it as, just as in a Google
search,

00:55:11.670 --> 00:55:14.270
they're looking at probability,
essentially of a good click.

00:55:14.270 --> 00:55:16.660
A click that then you didn't come back and

00:55:16.660 --> 00:55:19.550
then click on something else cuz the first
click didn't work.

00:55:19.550 --> 00:55:22.510
They wanted to actually solve your query.

00:55:22.510 --> 00:55:25.400
And we have a similar one which is, did
you, how much did you watch and

00:55:25.400 --> 00:55:26.590
did you get something you watch.

00:55:26.590 --> 00:55:31.190
So it's trying to look for things in your
rewind behavior and

00:55:31.190 --> 00:55:34.640
your propensity to try different things,
to continue with it.

00:55:34.640 --> 00:55:37.560
Did you watch five episodes in a row or
just one episode?

00:55:37.560 --> 00:55:39.140
Things like that.

00:55:39.140 --> 00:55:41.970
They give us a, a sense of intensity and
desire.

00:55:41.970 --> 00:55:45.710
And then that fuels algorithmic changes to
try to

00:55:45.710 --> 00:55:47.760
figure out what are you going to enjoy
most.

00:55:47.760 --> 00:55:49.820
So the idea is you open Netflix.

00:55:49.820 --> 00:55:52.890
And there's, like, ten things you're just
dying to watch because we've got

00:55:52.890 --> 00:55:54.020
the right ten things showing.

00:55:55.780 --> 00:55:56.310
&gt;&gt; Interesting.

00:55:56.310 --> 00:55:56.944
&gt;&gt; Last question.

00:55:56.944 --> 00:55:57.970
&gt;&gt; Let's do one more question.

